From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:46:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: optionally publish a render command
 behind a running one

The G16 backend publishes one command at a time: the next is built only
after the previous one's full receipt. On the J604 every render command
then pays about 0.86 ms that is not GPU work (the first firmware event
arrives about 0.66 ms after publication, while the TA stage takes about
11 us), and a desktop frame of eight or nine render passes no longer fits
in 8.33 ms. The G15 backend keeps a second prefix queued behind a running
one, and its firmware takes such a record within microseconds.

Add g16_flights (runtime 0644, default 1). At 2 a render command may be
published behind running render commands when every running flight is a
healthy render-only prefix, the render allocators are past their priming
prefix, the command's own exact prior-command dependencies are met, and
both submit pipes have room. Its records are posted behind the unconsumed
ones, and the pass slot lifetime keeps the two flights on different slots.

The oldest flight's receipt then cannot use exact equality on the shared
stamps, queue cursors, submit-pipe cursors and notifier records, which the
later flight moves on: with a flight behind it, it is proven when the
stamps reached its targets and every cursor passed its entries within the
newest publication. Recovery notifications are matched against every
flight. With g16_flights at 1 nothing changes.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
(cherry picked from commit dc4950f48bf8f5738749c07322672f3fe50f7883)
